        <div class="row py-3">
            <div class="col-8 align-self-start mx-5">
                <p>I was raised in Montana, where nature was always a big part of my life. I grew up camping, fishing, rock climbing, floating, disk golfing, and generally spending a lot of time outdoors. As a teenager, following a series of unfortunate events, I began avoiding family time outdoors. The details are not of importance, but I became intimately acquainted with mental/behavioral health counseling and spent three months away from my family receiving intensive care.</p>
                <p>I was so blessed to have a counselor who was not only empathetic and understanding, but who saw my potential, and had hope for my future, even when I did not. Because of this, they challenged me to be a better version of myself. Insert any number of “I feel called out” memes, but confronting our BS tends to be uncomfortable, especially when someone else is pointing it out. Without some challenge, counseling is just paying someone to be your agreeable friend. The compassion, directness, and encouragement I received informs my approach as a counselor today.</p>
                <p>When I returned home, I had a very short leash. My parents permitted me to hike a trail that was only a couple of blocks away. While reintegrating into the “real world”, preparing for graduation, and figuring out who I was, this path became a friend, a guide, and a place of solace for me to reflect each day and process the overabundance of human emotion I was experiencing.</p>
                <p>After I graduated from high school, I followed a curiosity for Buddhist philosophy that was ignited while receiving intensive care. It led me to Naropa University in Boulder, CO. Here, I went an even more alternative route than just attending a Buddhist-inspired university. I took the “build-your-own” degree approach and received my bachelor’s in interdisciplinary studies. The areas where I focused my studies were psychology of health and healing, peace and conflict studies, and environmental studies. I took classes in permaculture design, yoga, herbalism, Traditional Chinese Medicine, wilderness survival skills, ecopsychology, human rights and law, anatomy, and so much more. The culmination of my studies was exploring the interrelationship between personal health, communal health, and ecological health, but I did not know how this would translate to the world outside of the “Boulder Bubble”.</p>
                <p>I always thought I would go back to school to become a mental health counselor, especially if things did not work out with my (unique) degree. I took some time off and worked all kinds of jobs, including operating a loader in a landscape supply yard. I eventually became a stay-at-home mom. It was during this time that I chose to return to my studies. This was a very challenging time in my life and I took the opportunity to continue my own therapeutic journey, finding a counselor to stand by my side as I navigated the challenges of parenthood, being a student, and eventually working full time on top of it all. As a counselor I believe in the power of therapy and that all of us, at one point or another, can benefit from engaging in this amazing process.</p>
                <p>While in my program at Adams State University in Alamosa, CO, I worked for the Spokane Tribe. I started as a case manager, which taught me the importance and skill required to build strong community relationships to provide the best possible care for those seeking support. Our organization noted a lot of referrals coming from the school district, but we did not have much of a relationship established. I worked with school administrators to develop a collaborative program. By the time I stopped working for the tribe we had mental health counselors in the school three days a week, psychoeducational groups at the MS/HS and the elementary school, and we offered quarterly professional development sessions to school staff. This was a huge success in the community and caught attention from other programs. We began offering organizational workshops and retreats to other tribal departments. While working on these projects I also offered counseling to individuals, including young children and adolescents. I worked to engage families in treatment and enjoyed working with couples when the opportunity arose. After graduating I wanted to take some time off and enjoy the summer before figuring out my next chapter. It was a causal comment from my own counselor saying, “If it doesn’t work out you could always start your own practice” and I realized the potential.</p>
                <p>Currently I enjoy spending time with my family and friends, in and out of doors, regular walks by the river, exploring new and old trails, experimenting in the kitchen, playing on the water in the summer, playing in the snow with my child in winter, crocheting, playing music, dancing, and getting to live out my dream. I love being a mental health counselor and member of a rich and diverse community of helping professionals in the profoundly beautiful, quirky, and dynamic Spokane area. I love being able to enjoy the bounties of nature, one way or another, every single day. I always hoped my bachelor’s degree would amount to something. I am deeply grateful to the people who make my dreams and this practice possible. It is my true pleasure to walk with you on this path of self-discovery, bearing witness to you reaching your goals and falling in love with yourself.</p>
